module Darklang.Cli.Docs.Deprecation

let content(): String =
  """# Deprecation

Author-initiated notes that a fn/type/value/trait/impl shouldn't be used anymore.
A deprecation is said at a NAME, about the content it holds then. Content is
shared -- `Probe.v = 5` is the same item as every other `= 5` -- so deleting or
deprecating one name leaves the others alone. `harmful` is the exception: it is
about the code, so it marks every name holding that code. A pure rename of the same hash is not a
deprecation (name changes go through `SetName`); a rewrite with
a replacement is. Removing a NAME is a different op again, `Unbind`: the name
stops resolving, the content stays reachable by hash, and nothing is said
about the content. A deprecated name still resolves; an unbound one does not.
No verb writes an `Unbind` yet; the package importer will.

## Three kinds

  superseded-by    Use a different item (different hash) instead.
                   Requires --replacement.
  harmful          Actively dangerous (security, correctness, data loss).
                   Runtime halts on invocation unless --allow-harmful is set.
                   FNS ONLY: the halt happens at a call, so there is nowhere
                   for a marked type or value to stop. Those are refused.
  obsolete         Don't use this anymore (catch-all; no halt, no replacement).

## Commands

  deprecate <fn|type|value> <name-or-hash> --kind <k> [options]
  delete    <fn|type|value> <name-or-hash> [options]
  undeprecate <name>                         # put it back on the shelf

`delete` is sugar for `deprecate --kind obsolete`, but it requires
--ignore-dependents when the target has live dependents. Prefer
`deprecate --kind superseded-by` or `deprecate --kind harmful` when a
replacement or runtime-halt is wanted.

## Options

  --kind <k>                    superseded-by, harmful, or obsolete
  --replacement <name-or-hash>  Required for --kind superseded-by
  -m, --message <text>          Free-form reason; shown in view and search
  --yes, -y                     Skip confirmation prompt
  --dry-run                     Print what would happen; emit no ops
  --json                        Print the result as JSON (see below)
  --ignore-dependents           (delete only) proceed despite live dependents
  --force                       (delete only) ignore dependents and skip confirmation

## JSON

`deprecate --json` and `delete --json` print one object:

  { "deprecated": "Acme.oldThing", "itemType": "fn", "kind": "obsolete",
    "replacement": "", "message": "", "names": ["Acme.oldThing"], "dryRun": false }

`names` is every name the deprecation reaches: the target alone, or for
`harmful` every name holding the code. Without
--yes (or --force) nothing is written and `dryRun` is true. A refusal (not found,
live dependents) prints nothing on stdout, says why on stderr, and exits 1.

## Runtime behaviour

Invoking a `harmful`-marked item raises `RuntimeError.DeprecatedItemHalted`.
Tests, sandboxes, and security research can opt out:

  run --allow-harmful fn <target> [args...]
  eval --allow-harmful <expr>

`obsolete` and `superseded-by` do not halt. They surface in `view` and `ls`.

## Examples

  # Replace one fn with another
  deprecate fn Darklang.Stdlib.List.oldMap \\
    --kind superseded-by --replacement Darklang.Stdlib.List.map_ \\
    -m "rewrite for perf" --yes

  # Mark something actively dangerous
  deprecate fn Darklang.Stdlib.Crypto.brokenHash \\
    --kind harmful -m "CVE-2026-XXXX" --yes

  # Take off the shelf (catch-all)
  delete fn Darklang.Legacy.oldThing -m "no longer maintained" --yes

  # Put it back
  undeprecate Darklang.Stdlib.List.oldMap

## Undeprecating

  undeprecate <name>      # or `restore`

No `--kind` and no item type: you know the name, and making you also name the
kind to UN-do something is a question asked because it's easy to implement.
It clears any kind, including `harmful`, so the fn runs again without
`--allow-harmful`.

Deprecating the last implementation of a trait for a type is the one deprecation
that can refuse a later save: nothing is left to discharge the trait, so a new
call that needs it fails the at-rest check with `MissingImpl`. Calls saved while
it was live keep the implementation they resolved against and go on working, so
the order is: deprecate, then move the callers.

Deprecation is said at the name, so if two names hold the same bytes,
deleting or deprecating one leaves the other as it was, and `deprecate` says so
in one line on stderr. `harmful` marks both, and names the others; undeprecating
a harmful item clears it at every name holding the code. Trait dispatch counts
content as deprecated only when no name holds it undeprecated.

## Visibility in package tree

By default, deprecated items are hidden from `ls` / `tree` / `search` unless
a non-deprecated item still references them. A deleted name whose code another
name still holds is hidden too: the other name is the one listed. Pass `--include-deprecated` to
see them anyway. `view` always shows the deprecation header when present.

## Notes

  * Deprecation is of the content a name held when it was said. If you update
    `Foo.bar` to a new hash later, the deprecation doesn't follow -- the new
    hash is its own thing. Re-deprecate it if you want.
  * Branch-chain visibility: deprecating on a child branch extends the parent;
    a child can un-deprecate what the parent deprecated.
  * `delete` refuses without `--ignore-dependents` if live dependents exist.
    Use plain `deprecate` to give them time to migrate.
"""
